---
// Cross-system IVM (after Figure 3 of the SIGMOD 2024 demo paper).
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <div class="scroll">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 900 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="xs-title xs-desc">
      <title id="xs-title">Cross-system IVM between DuckDB and PostgreSQL</title>
      <desc id="xs-desc">
        The regional_totals view in DuckDB is defined over the sales table in an attached PostgreSQL instance.
        Updates to sales are captured in a delta table there. OpenIVM sends compiled queries to PostgreSQL, the
        incremental result flows back into the view's delta in DuckDB, and is merged into the view.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <marker id="xs-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" class="head"></path>
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <rect class="box input" x="10" y="30" width="200" height="132" rx="10"></rect>
      <text class="mono" x="24" y="54">CREATE MATERIALIZED</text>
      <text class="mono" x="24" y="72">VIEW regional_totals AS</text>
      <text class="mono" x="24" y="90">SELECT region,</text>
      <text class="mono" x="38" y="108">SUM(amount) AS total</text>
      <text class="mono" x="24" y="126">FROM p.sales</text>
      <text class="mono" x="24" y="144">GROUP BY region;</text>

      <rect class="engine" x="260" y="20" width="220" height="232" rx="16"></rect>
      <text class="eng" x="370" y="48">DuckDB</text>
      <text class="role" x="370" y="274">analytical engine</text>
      <rect class="box" x="292" y="80" width="156" height="46" rx="9"></rect>
      <text class="t" x="370" y="108">regional_totals</text>
      <rect class="box delta" x="292" y="176" width="156" height="46" rx="9"></rect>
      <text class="t" x="370" y="204">Δ regional_totals</text>

      <rect class="engine" x="620" y="20" width="220" height="232" rx="16"></rect>
      <text class="eng" x="730" y="48">PostgreSQL <tspan class="dim">p</tspan></text>
      <text class="role" x="730" y="274">attached transactional engine</text>
      <rect class="box" x="652" y="80" width="156" height="46" rx="9"></rect>
      <text class="t" x="730" y="108">sales</text>
      <rect class="box delta" x="652" y="176" width="156" height="46" rx="9"></rect>
      <text class="t" x="730" y="204">Δ sales</text>

      <g class="flow">
        <path d="M210 90H256" marker-end="url(#xs-arrow)"></path>
        <path d="M480 60H616" marker-end="url(#xs-arrow)"></path>
        <path d="M652 199H452" marker-end="url(#xs-arrow)"></path>
        <path d="M370 176V130" marker-end="url(#xs-arrow)"></path>
        <path d="M730 126V172" marker-end="url(#xs-arrow)"></path>
        <path d="M890 103H812" marker-end="url(#xs-arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="lbl" x="548" y="52" text-anchor="middle">compiled queries</text>
      <text class="lbl pink" x="552" y="190" text-anchor="middle">incremental result</text>
      <text class="lbl" x="378" y="157">merge</text>
      <text class="lbl" x="738" y="157">capture</text>
      <text class="lbl" x="886" y="94" text-anchor="end">updates</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Because the output is SQL, the maintenance steps can run where the data lives. Here PostgreSQL takes the
    transactional writes and DuckDB keeps the analytical view fresh. Adapted from the SIGMOD 2024 demo paper.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .diagram {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 680px;
    height: auto;
  }
  .engine {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--surface) 60%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--border);
  }
  .eng {
    font: 600 14px var(--font-sans);
    fill: var(--text);
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .dim {
    fill: var(--faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  .role {
    font: 400 11.5px var(--font-mono);
    fill: var(--faint);
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .box {
    fill: var(--surface-2);
    stroke: var(--border-strong);
  }
  .box.delta {
    stroke: var(--insert);
  }
  .box.input {
    fill: var(--bg);
  }
  .t {
    font: 600 13px var(--font-mono);
    fill: var(--text);
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .mono {
    font: 500 11.5px var(--font-mono);
    fill: var(--accent);
  }
  .flow path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--faint);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
  }
  .head {
    fill: var(--faint);
  }
  .lbl {
    font: 400 11px var(--font-mono);
    fill: var(--faint);
  }
  .lbl.pink {
    fill: var(--insert);
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    max-width: 90ch;
  }
</style>
